Expected elements: MISSING — Decoded input: the 'supply an ABI' action

That is finding #1, it is P1, and my rating is 4. Four of the five iteration changes landed on this view. Age (`AGE / No timestamp published`, y≈1006) states the absence instead of inventing a time. Type (`TYPE / public`, y≈969) is a labelled row. The full hash carries a resting affordance — bordered box at (505–921, 278–309) plus caption. The provenance sentences now span the grid and set flush left, though flush to the label gutter (x≈528) rather than to the badge they explain (x≈688) — L2's call. Fee/cost is base ten (`42000 / 100000 mana (FeeJuice)`), no hex word anywhere. Breadcrumb reads `demo` and the badge reads `Synthetic demo data`: the URL move introduced no contradiction, and exactly one provenance marker is present. The note card at y≈1478 no longer promises a remedy it cannot give — but the section still shows raw bytes with no control that supplies an ABI, so the element remains unsatisfied.

In register terms the page is a credible 2026 explorer surface. What the new elements cost is primitive discipline: the copy affordance wears the nav resolver's input costume, the Age statement wears the status chips' costume, and monospace — the face that means "this is chain data" — now sets five strings of ordinary English.

Highest-priority fixes: add the ABI control; give absence-of-data its own treatment, distinct from the Canonical/Finality chips.

Rating: 4/10

